      "id": "sol.ahsme-1975-p30.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Set \\(w=\\cos36^\\circ\\) and \\(y=\\cos72^\\circ.\\) The double-angle identities give \\[ y=2w^2-1,\\qquad w=1-2y^2. \\] Adding yields \\[ \\begin{aligned} w+y&=2(w^2-y^2)\\\\ &=2(w-y)(w+y). \\end{aligned} \\] Since \\(w+y\\ne0,\\) division gives \\(w-y=\\frac{1}{2}.\\) Thus \\(x=\\frac{1}{2}.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Let \\(w=\\cos36^\\circ\\) and \\(y=\\cos72^\\circ\\)",
        "Use \\(y=2w^2-1\\) and \\(w=1-2y^2\\), then add the equations"
      ],
